# Proton Mail third-party client registration dossier

Status: blocked on provider authorization.

Evidence reviewed: 2026-09-30.

## Decision

`protonmail-ai` will not enable direct live Mail authentication until Proton
publishes or issues an identity explicitly intended for third-party Mail
software. The SDK fallback `Other`, a guessed `external-mail` value, and any
Proton-owned first-party client ID are not acceptable substitutes.

This document is a registration dossier, not evidence of approval. Any identity
named only as an example remains unusable for live authentication until Proton
confirms it.

## Public Proton position

Proton's current Mail support documentation directs desktop third-party email
clients to Proton Mail Bridge for receiving and managing mail. Proton's Android
support page separately says Proton Mail does not integrate with third-party
email clients on Android.

SMTP Submission is a narrower exception. It lets supported paid/custom-domain
setups send mail from business applications or devices, but Proton explicitly
contrasts it with IMAP access and points receiving clients to Bridge.

Relevant public pages:

- `https://proton.me/support/clients-supported-bridge`
- `https://proton.me/support/why-you-need-bridge`
- `https://proton.me/support/mail-android`
- `https://proton.me/support/smtp-submission`

No public Mail developer portal, client-registration flow, Mail SDK integration
policy, or `external-mail` client identity was found during the 2026-09-30
review.

## Production evidence

A secret-free probe sent `POST /auth/v4/sessions` to the Mail production API
with `x-pm-appversion: Other`. Proton returned HTTP 422, API code 8004,
`Operation not allowed`.

The probe used no account identity or credential. Its temporary response was
inspected only for status/error metadata and immediately deleted.

This result means the SDK's unversioned `Other` fallback is useful protocol
evidence but does not establish production authorization for this project.

## Drive precedent, not Mail permission

Proton Drive's public SDK currently permits personal, non-commercial
experimentation under explicit usage guidelines while stating that commercial
or production third-party apps are not yet supported. Its README requires an
honest `x-pm-appversion` such as
`external-drive-{name}@{semver}-{channel}` and forbids masquerading as an
official Proton client.

The Drive README also states that non-compliant clients may be limited or
blocked and points affected third-party developers to Proton support. It says
commercial/production third-party apps are not yet supported and explicitly
lists authentication/login flows and session management outside the SDK scope.
This is a useful identity-policy precedent, not a Mail authentication recipe or
a grant of Mail API access.

Pinned Drive evidence:

- repository: `https://github.com/ProtonDriveApps/sdk`
- revision: `f28a93ceaeb787283fe287e8c18b11e4cc7aad3a`
- file: `README.md`

## Fork child-client validation evidence

WebClients has two relevant validation paths. A caller-supplied `clientId` on
the generic fork URL is limited to known platform prefixes such as `web`, `ios`,
`android`, `linux`, `windows`, `macos`, and `browser`; the API then rejects IDs
that do not exist.

The desktop QR path is different. Proton Drive's SDK builds
`/desktop/login?app=drive&pv=3#payload=...` with `external-drive` inside the QR
payload. Payload version 3 uses a 12-byte AES-GCM IV and authenticated context
`fork`; versions 1 and 2 use the legacy 16-byte IV, with `fork` AAD added in v2.

The pinned WebClients desktop handler deserializes that payload and requires the
child client ID to contain the selected product name. It then uses the
sign-in-with-another-device push flow.

Pinned evidence:

- WebClients revision: `74773f0b10cc31d32e43e82c061da5d75fef96ce`
- WebClients file:
  `applications/account/src/app/content/fork/handleDesktopFork.ts`
- WebClients file: `packages/shared/lib/authentication/fork/validation.ts`
- Drive SDK revision: `f28a93ceaeb787283fe287e8c18b11e4cc7aad3a`
- Drive SDK file: `incubating/account/js/src/authWeb.ts`

This makes the desktop QR flow the strongest public precedent for the intended
Mail handoff. It still does not prove that an `external-mail` backend identity
exists. Provider approval must confirm whether third-party Mail should use
`/desktop/login?app=mail&pv=3#payload=...`, the exact child client ID, and the
backend permissions attached to that identity.

## Public contact paths

Proton's support form is at `https://proton.me/support/contact`, and Proton
publishes `mail@support.proton.me` as an email alternative. The Proton Partners
Program explicitly invites product partners exploring integrations and
co-innovation and publishes `partners@proton.me`.

The partners page is `https://proton.me/business/partners`. These are contact
paths only; their existence is not evidence that a Mail API integration will be
approved.

## Registration request

A request to Proton should identify the project as open-source third-party
software and state the security boundaries before asking for access. It should
include the production `Other` rejection so Proton can distinguish a client-ID
policy issue from an account-login problem.

The request should ask Proton to confirm all of the following:

- whether third-party software may use Mail's direct authenticated protocol;
- the exact allowed `x-pm-appversion` shape for this project;
- the exact child client ID allowed in session-fork QR/manual-code framing;
- whether `POST /auth/v4/sessions` and `GET /auth/v4/sessions/forks` are
  permitted for that identity;
- the production API origin and any required user-agent or anti-abuse metadata;
- required retry, backoff, concurrency, event-sync, and rate-limit behavior;
- supported account/plan types and whether personal projects are permitted;
- token refresh, logout, remote revocation, and identity-rotation requirements;
- whether the project may access message, conversation, label, draft, send,
  attachment, and event endpoints through the resulting session;
- whether Proton offers a supported SDK or compatibility contract for Mail;
- the support/notification path for protocol or identity changes.

## Request template

Subject: Third-party Proton Mail client identity for session-fork integration

`protonmail-ai` is an open-source third-party project exploring a local Mail
integration. It does not collect Proton passwords or second factors, automate a
browser, copy browser sessions, or identify as an official Proton client.

We are evaluating Proton's session-fork flow so the user can approve access on a
Proton-controlled authenticated surface. A secret-free production bootstrap
using the Mail SDK fallback `x-pm-appversion: Other` returned HTTP 422 with
API code 8004 (`Operation not allowed`), so we have kept live access disabled
rather than
substituting a first-party client ID.

Does Proton offer an authorized third-party Mail app-version/client ID or a
registration process for this use case? If so, please confirm the allowed
`x-pm-appversion`, fork child client ID, API origin, permitted fork endpoints,
rate-limit/retry requirements, supported Mail capabilities, plan restrictions,
and token refresh/revocation requirements.

If direct third-party Mail API access is not supported, confirmation of that is
also useful so the project can keep the route disabled and document the
supported alternative accurately.

## Repository authorization gate

The adapter keeps one typed provider-authorization policy. Its current snapshot
is `Blocked`, records this dossier as the evidence reference, and contains no
approved identity fields. `ProviderProfile::live_auth_supported()` is derived
from whether that snapshot can produce a complete validated identity; it is not
a standalone boolean that can be enabled independently.

Changing only the internal status to `Approved` is insufficient. A future record
must also provide app version, child client ID, API base, fork payload version,
desktop-login path, approval reference, and approval date. Until all of those
validate, the public
snapshot itself continues to report `Blocked`.

Candidate validation still rejects `Other`, known first-party Mail identities,
malformed framing values, and unversioned app identities. Approval metadata
additionally requires an HTTPS API base, fork payload version 1 through 3, a
path beginning with `/`, date-shaped review/approval fields, and non-empty
whitespace-free evidence references.

The external identity regression test pins the blocked snapshot to this
versioned dossier and review date so code and provider evidence cannot drift
silently.

Every outbound auth `RequestSpec` also carries a `ProviderRequestIdentity`.
`SdkModel` is explicitly not live-authorized and carries only the SDK fallback
`Other`. The `Approved` request-identity class has no public constructor; it is
created only from an `ApprovedMailIdentity` emitted by the validated provider
authorization snapshot. That request identity binds both the approved
`x-pm-appversion` and approved API base; raw getters remain private to the
adapter so external transports must use the fail-closed live accessors.

## Approval evidence required by this repository

The P0 blocker may close only with provider evidence that identifies the allowed
third-party identity and scope. A support or partner response that merely says
"try Bridge" or "use SMTP" does not authorize direct Mail session access.

Before live code is enabled, the accepted evidence must be recorded without
private account data and must answer the app-version, child-client-ID, endpoint,
rate-limit, capability, and revocation questions above. The resulting identity
must pass the repository candidate validator and must not equal a known
first-party Mail identity.

A redacted anonymous-session bootstrap must then succeed under the approved
identity. Session-fork allocation must succeed under the same identity before
the policy may become `Approved` and `ProviderProfile::live_auth_supported()`
may return `true`.
